inside this brussels monolith the  european commission has been planning  coming up with its answer to the most  enduring political problem of brexit  what to do about northern ireland  because the nation is in a unique  position still part of the european  single market but also freely trading  goods with the rest of britain  it's a complex new status and everyone  agrees it's created problems with goods  moving in and out  and then there's this the european court  of justice the brexit minister lord  frost says it should have no power over  northern ireland the eu disagrees very  strongly  today brussels chief negotiator offered  a string of compromises but there was no  talk of removing the power of the court  i believe that what we presented today  is such an appealing picture that we  would really focus all our constructive  and creative energy  how to make this  as good as possible  for the people and businesses in  northern ireland and i hope  that  lord frost will join me in that effort  the european commission is proposing to  cut 80 percent of checks made on a  variety of british goods heading into  northern ireland  this includes items such as  confectionery dairy products fish and  yes cumberland sausages  the plan would also ensure the free flow  of medicines between great britain and  northern ireland  while customs paperwork will be halved  and more goods would be declared low  risk meaning they could be transported  faster  but the oversight of the european court  of justice would remain  problem with the the protocol at the  moment is that eu law is  with the ecj uh as the enforcer of it uh  is applied in northern ireland without  any sort of democratic process  so that i think has to change  the european commission has made some  significant concessions to such an  extent that some people in this building  think they've gone too far that they've  been too generous to the british  government what that means is that most  people agree there's not much wriggle  room left one senior diplomat told me he  considered this offer to be a take it or  leave it deal  on the other side of the border they are  keen on a deal  if everyone is operating in good faith  and if the focus is on addressing  disruption in trade between northern  ireland and great britain  then these proposals address the problem  and respect the treaties we all agreed  to  everyone agrees they want this sorted  but as every brexit veteran knows the  hunt for a compromise can go down to the  wire  adam parsons sky news brussels
 

